00:04:30.166 --> 00:04:33.120
So I'll just give you a couple
of examples of sort of

00:04:33.121 --> 00:04:35.620
really common
randomized algorithms

00:04:35.621 --> 00:04:37.910
that are used in machine
learning at data science.

00:04:37.911 --> 00:04:40.020
Probably the most well-known one

00:04:40.021 --> 00:04:41.565
is stochastic gradient ascent.

00:04:41.566 --> 00:04:43.850
You start gradient ascent
that is already pretty simple.

00:04:43.851 --> 00:04:45.770
I'm trying to
optimize a function in

00:04:45.771 --> 00:04:48.675
every step I walk in the
direction of steepest let's say,

00:04:48.676 --> 00:04:49.870
and I eventually converge

00:04:49.871 --> 00:04:51.475
towards a minimum
or a local minimum.

00:04:51.476 --> 00:04:53.885
Stochastic gradient
ascent I just say,

00:04:53.886 --> 00:04:55.610
I don't want to compute
a full gradient.

00:04:55.611 --> 00:04:57.620
Maybe I just want to
look at one data point

00:04:57.621 --> 00:04:59.740
at a time and compute
a gradient based of that.

00:04:59.741 --> 00:05:01.010
Some really course estimate of

00:05:01.011 --> 00:05:03.520
the gradient which is
correct in expectation.

00:05:03.521 --> 00:05:05.390
So I have this
gradient tilde which is

00:05:05.391 --> 00:05:07.700
correct in expectation but
might be really far off,

00:05:07.701 --> 00:05:08.860
and I might not really

00:05:08.861 --> 00:05:10.705
correlate that
strongly as gradient.

00:05:10.706 --> 00:05:11.990
I can still take small

00:05:11.991 --> 00:05:13.300
enough steps in
these directions and

00:05:13.301 --> 00:05:16.455
eventually find that I
convert to a minimum.

00:05:16.456 --> 00:05:18.640
This is a hugely
applied algorithm.

00:05:18.641 --> 00:05:19.940
Why is it so popular?

00:05:19.941 --> 00:05:21.870
One it's applicable
to a really wide range

00:05:21.871 --> 00:05:23.845
of problems just like
gradient ascent is.

00:05:23.846 --> 00:05:26.080
You can sort of throw it at
any optimization problem.

00:05:26.081 --> 00:05:27.460
You have to see what happens.

00:05:27.461 --> 00:05:29.290
It's really naturally
distributed.

00:05:29.291 --> 00:05:30.790
It's naturally
streaming the steps

00:05:30.791 --> 00:05:32.155
that you take are really cheap.

00:05:32.156 --> 00:05:33.920
And this is a really
nice thing in practice.

00:05:33.921 --> 00:05:35.480
You don't have
these big expensive steps.

00:05:35.481 --> 00:05:38.310
You have really cheap many steps
going on at once.

00:05:38.311 --> 00:05:41.060
And we also have some
theoretical understanding of it.

00:05:41.061 --> 00:05:42.550
At least for convex functions,

00:05:42.551 --> 00:05:44.600
we don't understand
exactly why it works for

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:46.855
all functions we apply
it to but by having,

00:05:46.856 --> 00:05:48.070
because it's simple enough we

00:05:48.071 --> 00:05:49.440
can really do some analysis on

00:05:49.441 --> 00:05:51.000
it and try to use

00:05:51.001 --> 00:05:52.550
this analysis to
understand what it's doing,

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:54.800
to understand how we like
to modify it and improve it

00:05:54.801 --> 00:05:58.000
and things like that.

00:05:58.001 --> 00:06:00.290
Another example are a mark
of chain Monte Carlo methods.

00:06:00.291 --> 00:06:02.805
It is more like
stochastic gradient ascent

00:06:02.806 --> 00:06:04.170
and you can throw them at tons

00:06:04.171 --> 00:06:06.140
of problems and
see what happens.

00:06:06.141 --> 00:06:08.170
They take really simple steps

00:06:08.171 --> 00:06:09.845
and try and sample
from the distribution.

00:06:09.846 --> 00:06:11.540
I just need to
say compute ratios

00:06:11.541 --> 00:06:12.560
against the distribution

00:06:12.561 --> 00:06:14.355
and take relatively
simple steps.

00:06:14.356 --> 00:06:15.490
And they're easy to implement.

00:06:15.491 --> 00:06:17.480
It's not, if I want
to implement one of

00:06:17.481 --> 00:06:20.195
these methods and number
of systems it's easy to do.

00:06:20.196 --> 00:06:24.100
And then my final example
which is quite close on

00:06:24.101 --> 00:06:25.765
my own researches random hashing

00:06:25.766 --> 00:06:28.575
and random feature hashing
and random projection.

00:06:28.576 --> 00:06:31.950
Basically this is I have
like a large data set.

00:06:31.951 --> 00:06:33.710
For whatever reason I
have many data points

00:06:33.711 --> 00:06:35.305
so meaning I have many features.

00:06:35.306 --> 00:06:37.110
And I randomly
compress this data

00:06:37.111 --> 00:06:39.320
set using some sort
of random hash.

00:06:39.321 --> 00:06:40.840
So what I mean is literally I

00:06:40.841 --> 00:06:42.830
randomly take
features or I randomly

00:06:42.831 --> 00:06:44.530
take examples and
I add them together

00:06:44.531 --> 00:06:47.010
maybe with some
appropriate wave function.

00:06:47.011 --> 00:06:49.910
Another way of viewing
this is I generate

00:06:49.911 --> 00:06:51.660
some random matrix pi from

00:06:51.661 --> 00:06:54.550
some distribution and I
compress my matrix with that.

00:06:54.551 --> 00:06:56.320
I take A and I multiply it by pi

00:06:56.321 --> 00:06:58.435
and apply this random
linear compression.

00:06:58.436 --> 00:07:00.260
And I hope that I get
something useful out.

00:07:00.261 --> 00:07:01.825
I hope that this
compression a tilde.

00:07:01.826 --> 00:07:04.200
I can then use in downstream
applications for things

00:07:04.201 --> 00:07:07.070
like clustering or PCA
and things like this.

00:07:07.071 --> 00:07:10.390
Again, this method applies
to a huge range of problems.

00:07:10.391 --> 00:07:11.660
It's sort of a very generic

00:07:11.661 --> 00:07:13.090
pre-processing method
almost like

00:07:13.091 --> 00:07:15.840
PCA in a way where
you can apply it,

00:07:15.841 --> 00:07:17.070
you can shrink
the size of your data

00:07:17.071 --> 00:07:18.170
and then you can
see what happens,

00:07:18.171 --> 00:07:19.490
what happens to your results.

00:07:19.491 --> 00:07:21.370
It's naturally
distributed is based on

00:07:21.371 --> 00:07:23.330
basically just doing
matrix multiplication

00:07:23.331 --> 00:07:24.730
which is a super easy thing to

00:07:24.731 --> 00:07:27.030
implement and is
widely implemented,

00:07:27.031 --> 00:07:28.245
basically implemented in

00:07:28.246 --> 00:07:30.120
any system that you're
going to be using.

00:07:30.121 --> 00:07:33.705
And again because it's
simple, I mean we have.

00:07:33.706 --> 00:07:35.660
It takes some math but we have

00:07:35.661 --> 00:07:37.300
good theoretical bounds
for it and we have

00:07:37.301 --> 00:07:39.150
some good theoretical
understanding of it.

00:07:39.151 --> 00:07:41.590
Based on sort of Johnson
Lindenstrauss type results in

00:07:41.591 --> 00:07:43.445
random matrix theory
we can actually sort of

00:07:43.446 --> 00:07:44.420
figure out what it's doing and

00:07:44.421 --> 00:07:46.145
why it might be working well.

00:07:46.146 --> 00:07:50.470
Okay. So also mentioned

00:07:50.471 --> 00:07:51.760
that all these
algorithms I mentioned

00:07:51.761 --> 00:07:53.310
and other randomized algorithms,

00:07:53.311 --> 00:07:54.775
these simple
randomized algorithms

00:07:54.776 --> 00:07:56.110
come with other advantages.

00:07:56.111 --> 00:07:57.880
They take small
local steps often.

00:07:57.881 --> 00:08:00.290
This is certainly true of
stochastic gradient methods.

00:08:00.291 --> 00:08:02.250
And this makes them sort of more

00:08:02.251 --> 00:08:04.120
tolerant to noise
and perturbations.

00:08:04.121 --> 00:08:05.485
For example there's
a lot of work on

00:08:05.486 --> 00:08:07.810
stochastic gradients and
asynchronous systems.

00:08:07.811 --> 00:08:09.060
And where you basically get

00:08:09.061 --> 00:08:10.510
perturbed behavior
because you don't

00:08:10.511 --> 00:08:14.395
synchronize the processors
running the steps very well.

00:08:14.396 --> 00:08:17.260
And you still see
good behavior in these systems.

00:08:17.261 --> 00:08:18.590
So these algorithms can be

00:08:18.591 --> 00:08:20.900
perturbed and seemed
to still work well.

00:08:20.901 --> 00:08:24.300
They all aggressively trade
approximation for efficiency.

00:08:24.301 --> 00:08:25.570
So all the ones that I mentioned

00:08:25.571 --> 00:08:27.060
are approximation algorithms.

00:08:27.061 --> 00:08:28.940
Randomization and sampling and

00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:32.035
sketching naturally lead to
approximation algorithms.

00:08:32.036 --> 00:08:33.580
And the good thing
is that often in

00:08:33.581 --> 00:08:34.650
data applications there's not

00:08:34.651 --> 00:08:36.475
some canonical answer
we're looking for.

00:08:36.476 --> 00:08:39.370
We have noise over the data
anyways and by trading off,

00:08:39.371 --> 00:08:41.090
by allowing ourselves
some approximation

00:08:41.091 --> 00:08:43.270
we can get much more
efficient algorithms.

00:08:43.271 --> 00:08:44.950
And then something that
I mentioned while I

00:08:44.951 --> 00:08:46.210
was talking about
these is a lot of

00:08:46.211 --> 00:08:48.070
these have relatively clean

