which I talked about before.

00:09:39.486 --> 00:09:41.050
I've been interested
in understanding how

00:09:41.051 --> 00:09:42.300
these methods can be applied

00:09:42.301 --> 00:09:43.625
to when you're out the problems.

00:09:43.626 --> 00:09:45.290
Canonical problems
and linear algebra

00:09:45.291 --> 00:09:47.310
which are useful
for data science.

00:09:47.311 --> 00:09:49.750
So one thing I've,

00:09:49.751 --> 00:09:51.690
a lot of times we
have bounds for

00:09:51.691 --> 00:09:54.155
stochastic gradient methods
for convex problems,

00:09:54.156 --> 00:09:56.420
things like solving
linear systems but we don't

00:09:56.421 --> 00:09:57.900
necessarily have bounds
for other problems

00:09:57.901 --> 00:09:59.330
for not convex problems.

00:09:59.331 --> 00:10:00.810
And what I've looked
at is combining

00:10:00.811 --> 00:10:01.870
these methods with tools from

00:10:01.871 --> 00:10:03.030
numerical linear algebra like

00:10:03.031 --> 00:10:05.000
Krylov Subsys methods and using

00:10:05.001 --> 00:10:07.070
these to sort of boost
the power of these algorithms,

00:10:07.071 --> 00:10:09.710
to try to solve things like
eigenvector approximation,

00:10:09.711 --> 00:10:11.250
spectrum approximation,

00:10:11.251 --> 00:10:13.125
certain forms of
regular as regression.

00:10:13.126 --> 00:10:14.710
To try to expand their use

00:10:14.711 --> 00:10:16.655
in linear algebra
and in data science.

00:10:16.656 --> 00:10:18.450
And this is required
turning back to

00:10:18.451 --> 00:10:20.740
these older tools like
Krylov Subsys methods and

00:10:20.741 --> 00:10:22.670
understanding how
do they play with

00:10:22.671 --> 00:10:23.990
noise and approximation and

00:10:23.991 --> 00:10:26.560
how are they robust
to this noise?

00:10:26.561 --> 00:10:29.630
Another piece of my work is

00:10:29.631 --> 00:10:31.755
understanding random
sketching methods for

00:10:31.756 --> 00:10:34.360
linear algebraic graph problems
and data problems

00:10:34.361 --> 00:10:37.310
like clustering in
low rank approximation.

00:10:37.311 --> 00:10:40.810
And a dim of my work here has
been trying to understand,

00:10:40.811 --> 00:10:43.170
given a random projection method

00:10:43.171 --> 00:10:44.605
or a random hashing method,

00:10:44.606 --> 00:10:46.260
how do I really characterize?

00:10:46.261 --> 00:10:47.950
I'm taking my matrix
and I'm randomly

00:10:47.951 --> 00:10:48.990
compressing it and then I'm

00:10:48.991 --> 00:10:50.300
going to do an operation on it.

00:10:50.301 --> 00:10:51.190
What is the right way to

00:10:51.191 --> 00:10:53.130
characterize what
the compression does

00:10:53.131 --> 00:10:56.165
to my matrix and how it
approximates the original data?

00:10:56.166 --> 00:10:57.790
And then if I can characterize

00:10:57.791 --> 00:10:59.490
this in a really
nice clean way then I

00:10:59.491 --> 00:11:00.830
can use this to give

00:11:00.831 --> 00:11:02.995
applications to lots
of downstream problems.

00:11:02.996 --> 00:11:05.625
So this I'm going to
focus on my work there.

00:11:05.626 --> 00:11:10.640
And then, a huge amount of
my work has been on sampling,

00:11:10.641 --> 00:11:11.540
and sampling is mostly

00:11:11.541 --> 00:11:12.780
what I'm going to
talk about today.

00:11:12.781 --> 00:11:15.160
Basically, trying to figure
out what is the right way

00:11:15.161 --> 00:11:17.865
to sample for linear
algebraic and data problems.

00:11:17.866 --> 00:11:20.980
And what I'm going to focus
on today is a line of work,

00:11:20.981 --> 00:11:22.950
which focuses specifically on

00:11:22.951 --> 00:11:24.615
important sampling methods in

00:11:24.616 --> 00:11:27.200
graph-out rhythms and
linear algebraic algorithms,

00:11:27.201 --> 00:11:28.920
in sort of a new class
of methods,

00:11:28.921 --> 00:11:30.900
which don't fall into the range

00:11:30.901 --> 00:11:32.250
of stochastic iterative methods.

00:11:32.251 --> 00:11:34.480
They're not quite the
same as random sketching,

00:11:34.481 --> 00:11:36.505
although there are
certain similarities,

00:11:36.506 --> 00:11:38.935
and they allow us to do
some really powerful things

00:11:38.936 --> 00:11:40.430
for canonical problems,

00:11:40.431 --> 00:11:43.080
like lowering approximation
or linear system solves.

00:11:43.081 --> 00:11:45.110
And then, if I have time,

00:11:45.111 --> 00:11:47.790
I'll touch back
on this connection

00:11:47.791 --> 00:11:50.000
to biological computation
and some work they've done on

00:11:50.001 --> 00:11:52.235
stochastic decision
making in ant colonies

00:11:52.236 --> 00:11:54.800
and how we can analyze
this behavior using

00:11:54.801 --> 00:11:55.860
a distributed algorithm like

00:11:55.861 --> 00:11:57.340
perspective and using a lot of

00:11:57.341 --> 00:11:58.390
the same tools we use to

00:11:58.391 --> 00:12:02.200
analyze the algorithms
that we ourselves designed.

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:06.830
Let me just briefly overview.

00:12:06.831 --> 00:12:08.330
So, as you noticed
in this last slide,

00:12:08.331 --> 00:12:11.000
a lot of my work is on
computational linear algebra and

00:12:11.001 --> 00:12:12.790
specific computational
linear algebra

00:12:12.791 --> 00:12:14.250
focused on data problems.

00:12:14.251 --> 00:12:16.360
So, let me just briefly overview

00:12:16.361 --> 00:12:18.630
the role of computational
linear algebra in data science

00:12:18.631 --> 00:12:20.890
and why I think it's
such an important area

00:12:20.891 --> 00:12:23.240
of algorithms to be looking at.

00:12:23.241 --> 00:12:26.090
Basically, data is
often represented as

00:12:26.091 --> 00:12:28.165
high dimensional
real valued vectors,

00:12:28.166 --> 00:12:29.260
and when this is the case,

00:12:29.261 --> 00:12:30.680
you can represent
your data set as

00:12:30.681 --> 00:12:33.270
a real value matrix with

00:12:33.271 --> 00:12:35.850
n rows representing
the data points

00:12:35.851 --> 00:12:37.840
and d columns
representing the features.

00:12:37.841 --> 00:12:39.780
And then transformations on

00:12:39.781 --> 00:12:42.695
this data naturally become
linear algebraic operations.

00:12:42.696 --> 00:12:45.620
So things like principle
component analysis,

00:12:45.621 --> 00:12:48.190
spectral methods, nonlinear
dimensionality reduction,

00:12:48.191 --> 00:12:50.140
kernel methods, linear models,

00:12:50.141 --> 00:12:51.960
these things are
all really bread

00:12:51.961 --> 00:12:53.870
and butter linear
algebraic operations

00:12:53.871 --> 00:12:56.395
that we run daily
on data problems,

00:12:56.396 --> 00:12:58.620
and they really come down
to figuring out how to do

00:12:58.621 --> 00:13:01.565
basic linear algebraic
computation faster.

00:13:01.566 --> 00:13:03.650
The paradigm of working with

00:13:03.651 --> 00:13:05.680
data through
matrices and through

00:13:05.681 --> 00:13:08.200
linear algebra is so
prevalent that often we

00:13:08.201 --> 00:13:09.410
have data that we

00:13:09.411 --> 00:13:10.840
can't right quite
represent in this form,

00:13:10.841 --> 00:13:12.880
and we actually try
to transform it,

00:13:12.881 --> 00:13:14.180
so things like
word embedding and

00:13:14.181 --> 00:13:16.510
Word2vec or spectral embeddings

00:13:16.511 --> 00:13:18.280
for graphs and
things like DeepWalk.

00:13:18.281 --> 00:13:20.110
This is such a
powerful computational

00:13:20.111 --> 00:13:21.200
paradigm that we often try to

00:13:21.201 --> 00:13:22.390
fit things into
it even when they

00:13:22.391 --> 00:13:24.180
don't seem to fit in initially,

00:13:24.181 --> 00:13:26.120
and this is because
we have a lot of

00:13:26.121 --> 00:13:27.590
really powerful
computational tools

00:13:27.591 --> 00:13:28.300
in linear algebra that

00:13:28.301 --> 00:13:31.960
we can use to our advantage
for processing data.

00:13:31.961 --> 00:13:36.100
In many applications of

00:13:36.101 --> 00:13:38.695
linear algebra boiled
down to two core problems.

00:13:38.696 --> 00:13:39.890
There's the really
core problems,

00:13:39.891 --> 00:13:41.430
like multiplying
matrices together or

00:13:41.431 --> 00:13:43.945
multiplying matrices
with vectors, but then,

00:13:43.946 --> 00:13:46.250
beyond those are sort of
two really core problems

00:13:46.251 --> 00:13:47.960
that at the heart of
a lot of what we do,

00:13:47.961 --> 00:13:49.160
and these are
the problems I've sort of

00:13:49.161 --> 00:13:50.225
focused on in my research.

00:13:50.226 --> 00:13:52.120
One of these is
solving linear systems.

00:13:52.121 --> 00:13:54.130
So solving linear systems,

00:13:54.131 --> 00:13:55.430
you might think of as applying

00:13:55.431 --> 00:13:58.410
the inverse of
a matrix to a vector.

00:13:58.411 --> 00:14:00.100
Another way to think
about this is you're just

00:14:00.101 --> 00:14:02.000
trying to solve
this minimization problem.

00:14:02.001 --> 00:14:05.330
I've given a matrix a I'm
given a target vector b

00:14:05.331 --> 00:14:06.650
and I'm trying to find

00:14:06.651 --> 00:14:08.060
a vector x that minimizes

00:14:08.061 --> 00:14:09.575
the difference
between a, x, and b.

00:14:09.576 --> 00:14:12.030
It's like ordinary least
squares regression.

00:14:12.031 --> 00:14:14.410
It's used as an
internal loop in things

00:14:14.411 --> 00:14:16.490
like iterative
reweighted least squares

00:14:16.491 --> 00:14:17.700
for generalized linear models.

00:14:17.701 --> 00:14:19.600
It's used inside of
interior point methods,

00:14:19.601 --> 00:14:22.785
and also, it's equivalent to
like a projection problem.

00:14:22.786 --> 00:14:26.350
The columns of A sort
of define a subspace,

00:14:26.351 --> 00:14:28.160
the column span of A.

00:14:28.161 --> 00:14:30.230
Ax is some vector
in that subspace,

00:14:30.231 --> 00:14:31.780
and what we're looking
for when we solve

00:14:31.781 --> 00:14:34.310
this problem is we're
looking for the vector Ax,

00:14:34.311 --> 00:14:35.630
the vector in that subspace,

00:14:35.631 --> 00:14:37.620
which is closest to
our input vector B.

00:14:37.621 --> 00:14:39.400
So, this is a really basic if

00:14:39.401 --> 00:14:41.210
you're working in
Euclidean space,

00:14:41.211 --> 00:14:44.350
and you're working in sort
of with vectorized data,

00:14:44.351 --> 00:14:46.540
this is a really basic operation
that you need to have.

00:14:46.541 --> 00:14:47.580
You're figuring out how close

00:14:47.581 --> 00:14:50.505
a vector is to a given subspace.

00:14:50.506 --> 00:14:53.125
And then, the other
problem that I've

00:14:53.126 --> 00:14:54.750
focused a lot on are computing

00:14:54.751 --> 00:14:56.370
the top singular vectors
of a matrix or

00:14:56.371 --> 00:14:58.940
computing the top
eigenvectors of a matrix.

00:14:58.941 --> 00:15:01.670
Basically, you want to find
the singular vectors which

00:15:01.671 --> 00:15:03.970
correspond to the largest
singular values in

00:15:03.971 --> 00:15:06.660
a matrix or how this
is often viewed at

00:15:06.661 --> 00:15:08.120
a high level as
you're trying to find

00:15:08.121 --> 00:15:10.410
the directions of
greatest variance in your data.

00:15:10.411 --> 00:15:12.775
And the reason you want to do
this is usually you end up,

00:15:12.776 --> 00:15:14.230
at some point you
end up projecting

00:15:14.231 --> 00:15:16.015
to those dimensions,
those directions.

00:15:16.016 --> 00:15:18.110
Basically, you end up
approximating your data set

00:15:18.111 --> 00:15:20.335
by just looking at the
directions of greatest variance.

00:15:20.336 --> 00:15:22.125
This is the basis behind PCA,

00:15:22.126 --> 00:15:24.975
spectral methods, and nonlinear
dimensionality reduction.

00:15:24.976 --> 00:15:26.990
One way that I like
to view this problem

00:15:26.991 --> 00:15:29.585
is we don't actually care
about finding the direct,

00:15:29.586 --> 00:15:31.360
well, sometimes, we do,
but we don't always care

00:15:31.361 --> 00:15:33.190
about finding the directions
of largest variance.

00:15:33.191 --> 00:15:34.400
We don't actually
care about finding

00:15:34.401 --> 00:15:36.840
V1 through VK exactly.

00:15:36.841 --> 00:15:38.340
What we often care
about is just that we

00:15:38.341 --> 00:15:40.170
capture most of
the variance overall.

00:15:40.171 --> 00:15:42.140
So,what we do is we
use these directions to

00:15:42.141 --> 00:15:44.590
generate something like
a low rank approximation.

00:15:44.591 --> 00:15:47.120
So, specifically, if I let V be

00:15:47.121 --> 00:15:48.550
the matrix whose columns are

00:15:48.551 --> 00:15:50.225
my top case singular vectors,

00:15:50.226 --> 00:15:52.720
those are the directions of
greatest variance in my data.

00:15:52.721 --> 00:15:55.670
If I project my matrix to them.

00:15:55.671 --> 00:15:59.680
So, I formed this
matrix AVV transpose.

00:15:59.681 --> 00:16:01.805
I've drawn this out here,
this is like a thin matrix

00:16:01.806 --> 00:16:05.495
AV times a wide
matrix V transpose.

00:16:05.496 --> 00:16:08.980
This is the minimum. This is
the rank K matrix which is

00:16:08.981 --> 00:16:11.480
closest to my matrix
in a bunch of norms,

00:16:11.481 --> 00:16:13.015
for example, the Frobenius

00:16:13.016 --> 00:16:15.725
norm where I'm just adding up
the sum of squared entries.

00:16:15.726 --> 00:16:17.650
Now, this is really often

00:16:17.651 --> 00:16:19.810
times why you want
to use computing

00:16:19.811 --> 00:16:21.225
the top casing benefactors

00:16:21.226 --> 00:16:22.870
because we find that
in practice a lot of

00:16:22.871 --> 00:16:24.440
matrices are close to

00:16:24.441 --> 00:16:26.480
low rank or at least they
have decaying spectrums,

00:16:26.481 --> 00:16:27.890
and you can approximate
them reasonably

00:16:27.891 --> 00:16:29.295
well by a low rank matrices.

00:16:29.296 --> 00:16:30.680
And so, this is exactly why

00:16:30.681 --> 00:16:32.025
you compute the similar vectors.

00:16:32.026 --> 00:16:33.360
You project to them
so that you can

00:16:33.361 --> 00:16:34.780
approximate
your matrix as well as

00:16:34.781 --> 00:16:38.175
possible with a really concise
low rank representation.

00:16:38.176 --> 00:16:40.020
Another way of
thinking about this is

00:16:40.021 --> 00:16:41.230
you just find a projection of

00:16:41.231 --> 00:16:44.425
your data that preserves
as much structure as possible.

00:16:44.426 --> 00:16:46.730
Basically, by hoping
that by getting

00:16:46.731 --> 00:16:49.010
a matrix as close as possible
to your original matrix,

00:16:49.011 --> 00:16:50.755
you preserve as much
structure as you can.

00:16:50.756 --> 00:16:52.430
So that's how I'm going to
think about this problem.

00:16:52.431 --> 00:16:53.190
I'm not going to focus on

00:16:53.191 --> 00:16:54.700
the singular vectors themselves,

00:16:54.701 --> 00:16:57.350
I'm going to focus on
sort of the main reason

00:16:57.351 --> 00:16:58.540
we care about
the singular vectors,

00:16:58.541 --> 00:17:01.885
which is computing
low rank approximation.

00:17:01.886 --> 00:17:05.355
Okay, so how do we
solve these problems?

00:17:05.356 --> 00:17:06.515
These are two problems,

00:17:06.516 --> 00:17:08.560
linear systems and
top singular vectors.

00:17:08.561 --> 00:17:10.210
They're sort of actually very

00:17:10.211 --> 00:17:12.470
closely tied together how
we solve these problems.

00:17:12.471 --> 00:17:14.650
They're sort of
the textbook direct methods,

00:17:14.651 --> 00:17:15.910
like things you
would learn about

00:17:15.911 --> 00:17:17.090
like Gaussian elimination,

00:17:17.091 --> 00:17:18.380
or it's Cholesky decomposition.

00:17:18.381 --> 00:17:20.245
There's a lot of
work in this area.

00:17:20.246 --> 00:17:22.345
These things take a long time.

00:17:22.346 --> 00:17:23.390
For an end-by-end matrix,

00:17:23.391 --> 00:17:24.800
they take n cubed time.

00:17:24.801 --> 00:17:26.885
You can't run these things
on large matrices.

00:17:26.886 --> 00:17:29.090
They are really important
algorithms because you often

00:17:29.091 --> 00:17:31.280
run them as sort of subroutines
and smaller matrix.

00:17:31.281 --> 00:17:33.410
All of my algorithms

00:17:33.411 --> 00:17:34.860
will use these sort
of direct methods of

00:17:34.861 --> 00:17:36.190
subroutines but will run them

00:17:36.191 --> 00:17:38.715
on small samples of
our original matrix.

00:17:38.716 --> 00:17:40.710
But you can't just go and

00:17:40.711 --> 00:17:43.150
run them on a large matrix,
it's just not possible.

00:17:43.151 --> 00:17:45.640
Iterative methods are things

00:17:45.641 --> 00:17:47.620
like conjugate gradient
for linear system,

00:17:47.621 --> 00:17:51.240
Lanczos methods, gradient
descent for linear systems.

00:17:51.241 --> 00:17:53.430
These things, basically,
their basic operation

00:17:53.431 --> 00:17:55.680
is doing a matrix
vector multiply,

00:17:55.681 --> 00:17:57.490
and they use some
iterative approach to

00:17:57.491 --> 00:17:59.130
try to get closer
to the solution to

00:17:59.131 --> 00:18:00.660
linear system or solution to

00:18:00.661 --> 00:18:02.430
some eigenvectors
with each iteration

00:18:02.431 --> 00:18:04.060
being a matrix vector multiply.

00:18:04.061 --> 00:18:05.490
These things are generally much

00:18:05.491 --> 00:18:06.740
more efficient than
direct methods.

00:18:06.741 --> 00:18:08.555
You can actually run
them on large matrices.

00:18:08.556 --> 00:18:09.860
You can distribute your matrix

00:18:09.861 --> 00:18:11.450
vector multiplies, but again,

00:18:11.451 --> 00:18:13.270
every multiplication that you

00:18:13.271 --> 00:18:15.435
perform is doing
a full pass over your data.

00:18:15.436 --> 00:18:17.300
So, there's still something,

00:18:17.301 --> 00:18:18.490
there's still a question of,

00:18:18.491 --> 00:18:19.450
is this really the best we can.

00:18:19.451 --> 00:18:20.980
Do or do we really need
to make a full pass

00:18:20.981 --> 00:18:23.860
over the data for
every single iteration.

00:18:23.861 --> 00:18:26.700
And so my work sort of

00:18:26.701 --> 00:18:28.640
falls in a third category
of algorithms which is

00:18:28.641 --> 00:18:31.560
only sort of recently come

00:18:31.561 --> 00:18:35.295
to- recently grown
up in some way.

00:18:35.296 --> 00:18:37.560
The methods here are randomized.

00:18:37.561 --> 00:18:39.310
So, things like
compressing your matrix

00:18:39.311 --> 00:18:41.565
with random hashing
or random projection,

00:18:41.566 --> 00:18:42.950
stochastic gradients, what I

00:18:42.951 --> 00:18:44.560
mentioned in random sampling,

00:18:44.561 --> 00:18:46.175
which I'm going to talk
about in this talk.

00:18:46.176 --> 00:18:48.500
These things are trying
to sort of access

00:18:48.501 --> 00:18:50.775
your matrix in
a very local or limited way,

00:18:50.776 --> 00:18:53.350
not to a full pass over
your matrix for every iteration,

00:18:53.351 --> 00:18:54.920
and they're trying
to exploit this to

00:18:54.921 --> 00:18:56.850
get faster run times.

00:18:56.851 --> 00:18:58.220
You can often get really fast,

00:18:58.221 --> 00:18:59.630
surprisingly fast run times,

00:18:59.631 --> 00:19:01.620
like one thing you see

00:19:01.621 --> 00:19:03.130
sometimes quoted is you can get

00:19:03.131 --> 00:19:04.915
run times of the form NNZA,

00:19:04.916 --> 00:19:07.290
so that's the number of
nonzeros in your matrix.

00:19:07.291 --> 00:19:10.270
So if I stored my matrix
as a sparse matrix,

00:19:10.271 --> 00:19:12.490
this would be the time it
takes me to read my matrix,

00:19:12.491 --> 00:19:14.050
just the list of entries.

00:19:14.051 --> 00:19:15.930
Plus, some lower order terms,

00:19:15.931 --> 00:19:18.230
like depending on
lower dimensional terms.

00:19:18.231 --> 00:19:20.265
So for low rank
approximation, for example,

00:19:20.266 --> 00:19:23.375
you can get run times of
the form order NNZA basically,

00:19:23.376 --> 00:19:25.500
order one reads of my matrix,

