00:22:34.151 --> 00:22:35.460
you're just throwing out thing,

00:22:35.461 --> 00:22:36.640
you're throwing out information.

00:22:36.641 --> 00:22:37.560
You're throwing out data points,

00:22:37.561 --> 00:22:39.030
you're throwing out features.

00:22:39.031 --> 00:22:42.455
And often the sample doesn't
well represent your matrix.

00:22:42.456 --> 00:22:44.830
So, a simple way to see this,

00:22:44.831 --> 00:22:46.480
is if I have a cluster dataset C

00:22:46.481 --> 00:22:48.725
with some imbalance
in my clusters.

00:22:48.726 --> 00:22:50.500
I might not know these clusters

00:22:50.501 --> 00:22:51.655
but sort of they fall into,

00:22:51.656 --> 00:22:53.470
my data falls into
these three clusters,

00:22:53.471 --> 00:22:56.420
and one of them is a lot
bigger than the other ones.

00:22:56.421 --> 00:22:58.290
Then if I do uniform sampling,

00:22:58.291 --> 00:23:00.090
I'm going to tend to get
more points in that cluster,

00:23:00.091 --> 00:23:01.470
and I can easily miss

00:23:01.471 --> 00:23:03.230
like all the points
in this third cluster.

00:23:03.231 --> 00:23:03.880
And for all I know,

00:23:03.881 --> 00:23:05.180
those are the points
that really matter.

00:23:05.181 --> 00:23:06.190
Maybe those are
the features that

00:23:06.191 --> 00:23:07.955
really contain
a lot of my noise.

00:23:07.956 --> 00:23:10.610
So uniform sampling
can be problematic.

00:23:10.611 --> 00:23:12.780
And for this reasons, there's
tons of work on figuring

00:23:12.781 --> 00:23:15.110
out well what should we do
instead of uniform sampling?

00:23:15.111 --> 00:23:17.200
There's sort of clustering,
and distance based

00:23:17.201 --> 00:23:19.200
methods, if you've
heard of them,

00:23:19.201 --> 00:23:20.810
there are these things
