different formulations,

00:35:29.071 --> 00:35:30.490
but what I'm going to
show you right now is

00:35:30.491 --> 00:35:32.050
like the sort of
cleanness formulation that

00:35:32.051 --> 00:35:33.380
we've come up with which

00:35:33.381 --> 00:35:35.230
is this thing called The
Ridge leverage score.

00:35:35.231 --> 00:35:36.510
This is what I'm
going to find my

00:35:36.511 --> 00:35:37.935
sampling probability to be.

00:35:37.936 --> 00:35:40.510
The actual form of this
doesn't matter too much,

00:35:40.511 --> 00:35:43.190
the point is it's
some score it's

00:35:43.191 --> 00:35:44.450
the probability
that I'm going to

00:35:44.451 --> 00:35:46.215
sample the higher row
of my matrix.

00:35:46.216 --> 00:35:47.855
It depends on that row,

00:35:47.856 --> 00:35:50.910
it depends on the matrix and
also depends on this lambda,

00:35:50.911 --> 00:35:52.890
this additive error that
I'm going to allow myself.

00:35:52.891 --> 00:35:54.420
It's basically
a quadratic form over

00:35:54.421 --> 00:35:57.400
the inverse of the Matrix
regularized by that lambda.

00:35:57.401 --> 00:36:00.000
The important properties
of this is one

00:36:00.001 --> 00:36:01.580
that it can be the sum
of these scores

00:36:01.581 --> 00:36:03.030
can be bounded as a function of

00:36:03.031 --> 00:36:04.950
lambda and as a function
of the Matrix A.

00:36:04.951 --> 00:36:06.870
Which is important because
the sum of the scores,

00:36:06.871 --> 00:36:08.070
remember I'm sampling each row

00:36:08.071 --> 00:36:09.420
independently with these scores,

00:36:09.421 --> 00:36:11.890
so sum of the scores is
my expected sample size.

00:36:11.891 --> 00:36:13.490
So if I'm on a FAST
algorithm I'm going to

00:36:13.491 --> 00:36:15.185
need some way to bound
some of these scores.

00:36:15.186 --> 00:36:18.385
The larger lambda is the
smaller the sum becomes.

00:36:18.386 --> 00:36:21.360
When lambda is zero, this is
for example when I need to

00:36:21.361 --> 00:36:24.240
do preconditioning or
approximate system solves,

00:36:24.241 --> 00:36:26.955
the sum of the scores is
at most the rank of my matrix.

00:36:26.956 --> 00:36:28.880
So as long as I

00:36:28.881 --> 00:36:31.240
select the number of rows
equal to the rate of my matrix,

00:36:31.241 --> 00:36:34.230
I get this thing with additive
error lambda equals zero.

00:36:34.231 --> 00:36:36.040
For low rank
approximation you can

00:36:36.041 --> 00:36:37.670
do things that are
a lot stronger.

00:36:37.671 --> 00:36:38.970
If you appropriately set

00:36:38.971 --> 00:36:40.825
lambda and I won't go
into the details here,

00:36:40.826 --> 00:36:42.410
you can show that the sum
of these scores is

00:36:42.411 --> 00:36:44.320
less than like
for example two K,

00:36:44.321 --> 00:36:46.735
where you're looking for
a rank K approximation.

00:36:46.736 --> 00:36:49.240
This means that when I
sample from my matrix,

00:36:49.241 --> 00:36:52.060
in expectation I get 2K rows.

00:36:52.061 --> 00:36:53.895
Meaning I get something like

00:36:53.896 --> 00:36:56.555
usually order K rows I
have this small matrix.

00:36:56.556 --> 00:36:58.090
I can perform an SVD on

00:36:58.091 --> 00:37:00.330
this matrix which is
reasonably inexpensive.

00:37:00.331 --> 00:37:03.280
It cost me maybe like
D times K square times.

00:37:03.281 --> 00:37:04.640
This is a lot faster
than doing an SVD

00:37:04.641 --> 00:37:06.110
in the original Matrix and I

00:37:06.111 --> 00:37:07.350
can get near optimal low rank

00:37:07.351 --> 00:37:09.130
approximation of
my original Matrix.

00:37:09.131 --> 00:37:10.830
So it's critical that
we're able to bound,

00:37:10.831 --> 00:37:12.210
we have good bounds
on the sum of

00:37:12.211 --> 00:37:14.560
these scores because that's
going to be our sample size.

00:37:14.561 --> 00:37:16.660
&gt;&gt; How is this
related to clustering

00:37:16.661 --> 00:37:17.790
make sure it's okay means

