00:57:59.281 --> 00:58:00.610
You can do it officially and you

00:58:00.611 --> 00:58:01.860
can get down to this core of

00:58:01.861 --> 00:58:02.960
the matrix which contains

00:58:02.961 --> 00:58:05.020
very few rows and
columns and from this,

00:58:05.021 --> 00:58:07.245
you can extract a near optimal
low-rank approximation.

00:58:07.246 --> 00:58:09.110
The details don't really matter,

00:58:09.111 --> 00:58:10.450
I thought that this
was surprising.

00:58:10.451 --> 00:58:11.700
The only structural property we

00:58:11.701 --> 00:58:13.530
exploit from the matrix
is that it's positive

00:58:13.531 --> 00:58:15.380
semi-definite and then
we don't need to read

00:58:15.381 --> 00:58:16.320
the whole matrix and we can get

00:58:16.321 --> 00:58:19.220
a near optimal
low-rank approximation.

00:58:19.221 --> 00:58:21.770
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:58:21.771 --> 00:58:23.995
&gt;&gt; Yes, so this is like,

00:58:23.996 --> 00:58:25.550
first of all this is our bounds.

00:58:25.551 --> 00:58:26.850
I don't know if it is
actually the right thing,

00:58:26.851 --> 00:58:28.690
but it probably is
at least one over epsilon.

00:58:28.691 --> 00:58:30.420
We have a lower bound
of one over epsilon

00:58:30.421 --> 00:58:33.105
and probably at least one
over epsilon squared.

00:58:33.106 --> 00:58:35.580
But even if for example,

00:58:35.581 --> 00:58:37.690
if you apply a constant factor,

00:58:37.691 --> 00:58:39.040
often you're seeing your tail of

00:58:39.041 --> 00:58:40.300
your matrix fall
off really quickly.

00:58:40.301 --> 00:58:42.240
So, you're not looking
for really good epsilon.

00:58:42.241 --> 00:58:43.430
You just care about getting most

00:58:43.431 --> 00:58:46.610
of the master matrix
and you're paying.

00:58:46.611 --> 00:58:51.280
So, I guess, I don't know,

00:58:51.281 --> 00:58:53.380
does this end at half past or.

00:58:53.381 --> 00:58:56.615
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we had better
quickly wrap up.

00:58:56.616 --> 00:58:58.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, why don't I wrap

00:58:58.501 --> 00:59:00.490
up on the linear
algebra section.

00:59:00.491 --> 00:59:01.725
I can take questions on it

00:59:01.726 --> 00:59:03.140
and then if people want to stay,

00:59:03.141 --> 00:59:04.260
I can talk about
the BIOS stuff or I

00:59:04.261 --> 00:59:05.460
can just skip it, we can decide.

00:59:05.461 --> 00:59:10.225
Okay? So, okay,
this is a summary.

00:59:10.226 --> 00:59:12.025
What was the right way
to select data points and

00:59:12.026 --> 00:59:14.300
features for linear
algebraic computation?

00:59:14.301 --> 00:59:17.790
We considered simple,
important sampling strategies.

00:59:17.791 --> 00:59:20.550
We defined like a very clear
spectral approximation goal

00:59:20.551 --> 00:59:24.045
and this led us to find
very clear probabilities.

00:59:24.046 --> 00:59:26.860
We then made this algorithmic
an efficient by

00:59:26.861 --> 00:59:28.770
bootstrapping
uniform sampling and

00:59:28.771 --> 00:59:30.850
we're getting some
pretty powerful results

00:59:30.851 --> 00:59:33.070
for kernel methods and for
PSD matrices and hopefully,

00:59:33.071 --> 00:59:34.860
we can get more powerful
results in areas

00:59:34.861 --> 00:59:38.160
where other randomized methods
don't really suffice.

00:59:38.161 --> 00:59:40.500
In this talk, I mostly talked

00:59:40.501 --> 00:59:42.170
about sampling,
one-shot sampling.

00:59:42.171 --> 00:59:44.525
I have a big matrix and I
want to approximate it once.

00:59:44.526 --> 00:59:45.470
But obviously, you can use

00:59:45.471 --> 00:59:46.660
sampling in a lot of other ways.

00:59:46.661 --> 00:59:48.870
So, an obvious one is a part of

00:59:48.871 --> 00:59:50.190
iterative methods
and understanding

00:59:50.191 --> 00:59:51.630
how you can use
sampling, for example,

00:59:51.631 --> 00:59:53.300
to speed up
second order methods,

00:59:53.301 --> 00:59:55.130
is a really big question in

00:59:55.131 --> 00:59:58.330
a sort of alternative way of
using it in linear algebra.

00:59:58.331 --> 01:00:02.280
You can use these methods,

01:00:02.281 --> 01:00:04.330
these iterative methods, to
give fast algorithms as I

01:00:04.331 --> 01:00:06.560
mentioned for lots of
linear algebraic problems.

01:00:06.561 --> 01:00:09.425
So, it's really interesting
area to look at.

01:00:09.426 --> 01:00:12.170
And also, we've been
interested recently in

01:00:12.171 --> 01:00:13.510
generalizing this word beyond

01:00:13.511 --> 01:00:14.950
basically in linear
algebraic problem,

01:00:14.951 --> 01:00:17.080
for example, sampling
Fourier features.

01:00:17.081 --> 01:00:19.780
We've used this specifically
for kernel approximation,

01:00:19.781 --> 01:00:20.790
but hopefully can be used in

01:00:20.791 --> 01:00:23.805
other Fourier
analysis computation

01:00:23.806 --> 01:00:25.940
and sampling for
Fourier techniques.

01:00:25.941 --> 01:00:28.580
And maybe for
other types of models.

01:00:28.581 --> 01:00:30.380
And the last thing
I'll mention is that

01:00:30.381 --> 01:00:31.760
I've been talking
this entire time about

01:00:31.761 --> 01:00:33.660
how important randomness is and

01:00:33.661 --> 01:00:36.110
approximation in these
algorithms I'm showing you.

01:00:36.111 --> 01:00:38.060
We don't really
actually understand it.

01:00:38.061 --> 01:00:39.720
I don't know that
this is really true.

01:00:39.721 --> 01:00:42.410
So, I'll tell you
one fact which is

01:00:42.411 --> 01:00:45.160
that any fast algorithm
on a matrix.

01:00:45.161 --> 01:00:47.380
So, basically anything
that runs in faster than

01:00:47.381 --> 01:00:49.230
order n cubed time or

01:00:49.231 --> 01:00:51.200
faster than fast matrix
multiplication time,

01:00:51.201 --> 01:00:52.630
if you want to think about that,

01:00:52.631 --> 01:00:54.670
uses one of two techniques.

01:00:54.671 --> 01:00:56.800
Either it makes
assumptions on the matrix,

01:00:56.801 --> 01:00:57.900
assume something like bounded

01:00:57.901 --> 01:00:59.720
condition number or
bounded eigenvalue bounds.

01:00:59.721 --> 01:01:01.560
This is how you get
classic iterative methods.

01:01:01.561 --> 01:01:04.060
Or it does what we're doing,
it uses randomization.

01:01:04.061 --> 01:01:05.530
It pays these one over epsilon

01:01:05.531 --> 01:01:07.710
squared or one over epsilon
to the fourth factors.

01:01:07.711 --> 01:01:09.390
All algorithms I
know of except for

01:01:09.391 --> 01:01:11.325
doing something like computing
the trace or something,

01:01:11.326 --> 01:01:12.920
for any non-trivial problem,

01:01:12.921 --> 01:01:14.610
they fall into one of

01:01:14.611 --> 01:01:17.810
these two paradigms and we
want to explain why this is.

01:01:17.811 --> 01:01:19.140
Is there anything
else we can do?

01:01:19.141 --> 01:01:20.190
Do we really need to be using

01:01:20.191 --> 01:01:21.700
randomization
approximation in order

01:01:21.701 --> 01:01:22.910
you get around assumptions?

01:01:22.911 --> 01:01:25.150
Or are there some class of
faster algorithms that we just

01:01:25.151 --> 01:01:27.500
don't know about yet?

01:01:27.501 --> 01:01:29.980
But a really simple
question, just a thought,

01:01:29.981 --> 01:01:31.530
it's not even known how to

01:01:31.531 --> 01:01:33.995
deterministically
compute an approximation

01:01:33.996 --> 01:01:35.410
of the top singular value of

01:01:35.411 --> 01:01:39.060
a matrix without computing
eigendecomposition.

01:01:39.061 --> 01:01:41.310
Is this really the
best we can do?

01:01:41.311 --> 01:01:43.490
Can you prove that it
should be the best we

01:01:43.491 --> 01:01:46.160
can do or can or are
we really stuck there?

01:01:46.161 --> 01:01:47.910
Okay, so I close on that and

01:01:47.911 --> 01:01:52.010
then I can take questions,
general questions.

01:01:54.900 --> 01:01:59.110
&gt;&gt; See if you could
do two minutes

01:01:59.111 --> 01:02:00.365
of strongest cool pictures

01:02:00.366 --> 01:02:01.865
of ants and stuff,
that would be.

01:02:01.866 --> 01:02:02.850
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I can do that.

01:02:02.851 --> 01:02:04.660
&gt;&gt; Just really quickly. I think

01:02:04.661 --> 01:02:07.430
everybody wants us to see it.

01:02:07.431 --> 01:02:10.260
&gt;&gt; Okay. I know
any algorithms aren't more

01:02:10.261 --> 01:02:14.060
boring than ants for a second.

01:02:14.061 --> 01:02:16.410
I'll just tell you exactly
the thing that we've studied.

01:02:16.411 --> 01:02:19.110
So, what we've been
interested in is understanding

01:02:19.111 --> 01:02:21.260
there's all these
biological systems that

01:02:21.261 --> 01:02:22.975
are essentially running
randomized algorithms.

01:02:22.976 --> 01:02:25.300
Ants essentially run
randomized algorithms,

01:02:25.301 --> 01:02:26.200
and not only do they run them,

01:02:26.201 --> 01:02:27.200
we know what they're running.

01:02:27.201 --> 01:02:29.410
Because biologists can
probe their behavior,

01:02:29.411 --> 01:02:31.240
and figure out how does
this ant make this decision?

01:02:31.241 --> 01:02:32.480
What's the input that causes

01:02:32.481 --> 01:02:33.985
them to make this run decision?

01:02:33.986 --> 01:02:36.290
But, biologists don't
really have a good way of

01:02:36.291 --> 01:02:37.430
analyzing to figure out how

01:02:37.431 --> 01:02:38.905
all of these things
fit together.

01:02:38.906 --> 01:02:45.825
So, the process that we've
been studying is basically,

01:02:45.826 --> 01:02:48.480
there's these certain ants,

01:02:48.481 --> 01:02:50.170
these rock ants, that basically,

01:02:50.171 --> 01:02:52.680
they have to do
this process of when

01:02:52.681 --> 01:02:53.930
their nest becomes too small

01:02:53.931 --> 01:02:55.360
because their colony grew a lot,

01:02:55.361 --> 01:02:57.310
or when their nest is
destroyed for some reason,

01:02:57.311 --> 01:02:58.755
they have to go find a new nest.

01:02:58.756 --> 01:03:00.825
And they have to do this
in a distributed way.

01:03:00.826 --> 01:03:02.340
So, there's no central
controller to say,

01:03:02.341 --> 01:03:03.905
"Look for a new nest
and move to it."

01:03:03.906 --> 01:03:06.150
So, the algorithm that
they run is first,

01:03:06.151 --> 01:03:09.040
some scout agents, some small
subset of the population.

01:03:09.041 --> 01:03:10.290
They notice that their nest is

01:03:10.291 --> 01:03:11.480
messed up for whatever reason,

01:03:11.481 --> 01:03:13.525
and they start going
looking for new nest sites.

01:03:13.526 --> 01:03:16.150
They assess these nest sites,
and they can do this,

01:03:16.151 --> 01:03:17.780
each individual ant
is complex enough

01:03:17.781 --> 01:03:19.545
to actually assess
the quality of a nest site.

01:03:19.546 --> 01:03:21.370
They can roughly
measure its size,

01:03:21.371 --> 01:03:22.990
they can measure
how much light is in there,

01:03:22.991 --> 01:03:24.480
so sort of how protected

01:03:24.481 --> 01:03:26.235
it is from the elements
and things like this.

01:03:26.236 --> 01:03:27.680
So you get some sort
of really coarse

01:03:27.681 --> 01:03:29.750
approximation to its quality.

01:03:29.751 --> 01:03:31.770
Both of these things are random.

01:03:31.771 --> 01:03:33.710
They're not doing some sort
of grid search or something,

01:03:33.711 --> 01:03:35.120
they're just randomly
walking around them,

01:03:35.121 --> 01:03:36.430
and when they see
a nest site, they assess

01:03:36.431 --> 01:03:38.510
its quality and this
triggers something.

01:03:38.511 --> 01:03:41.485
If it's above a certain
threshold, they make a decision.

01:03:41.486 --> 01:03:43.550
This decision is that
they decide to recruit

01:03:43.551 --> 01:03:45.905
other scouts to try to
assess this nest quality.

01:03:45.906 --> 01:03:47.170
So, if the nest is
good enough, they

01:03:47.171 --> 01:03:48.700
go back to the home nest.

01:03:48.701 --> 01:03:50.230
They randomly walk around,

01:03:50.231 --> 01:03:51.740
they randomly bump
into another ant,

01:03:51.741 --> 01:03:53.620
and then they try to
recruit it to actually doing

01:03:53.621 --> 01:03:56.475
its own independent
assessment of the nest.

01:03:56.476 --> 01:03:58.640
It does its own
random assessment.

01:03:58.641 --> 01:04:00.410
They keep doing this process,

01:04:00.411 --> 01:04:01.990
eventually it builds up to

01:04:01.991 --> 01:04:03.680
a sort of quorum
threshold of ants that

01:04:03.681 --> 01:04:07.605
actually think this new nest
is like a good nest.

01:04:07.606 --> 01:04:11.035
This is also a random process
that's why I have it up.

01:04:11.036 --> 01:04:14.220
What they then do, is
they decide on this nest.

01:04:14.221 --> 01:04:15.510
So, if there's
enough scouts that

01:04:15.511 --> 01:04:16.840
are somehow recruiting
to this nest,

01:04:16.841 --> 01:04:18.360
they can actually sense this.

01:04:18.361 --> 01:04:20.220
It triggers something
in their mind

01:04:20.221 --> 01:04:21.885
where they say this
is our new home,

01:04:21.886 --> 01:04:23.550
and then what they
do is they initiate

01:04:23.551 --> 01:04:25.035
a decision making process.

01:04:25.036 --> 01:04:26.380
What they do is
they actually move

01:04:26.381 --> 01:04:29.555
the entire rest of the colony
over to this new nest site.

01:04:29.556 --> 01:04:31.900
And this is sort of
necessary because a lot

01:04:31.901 --> 01:04:34.050
of the agents in
the colony are on mobile.

01:04:34.051 --> 01:04:35.430
So, the queen is in mobile,

01:04:35.431 --> 01:04:37.040
the brood is in mobile,
there's a lot of ants

01:04:37.041 --> 01:04:38.570
that for whatever reason
aren't understood.

01:04:38.571 --> 01:04:39.990
They just sit around
and don't do anything.

01:04:39.991 --> 01:04:42.025
What they do is they
literally go back to the nest,

01:04:42.026 --> 01:04:43.520
they pick up
the other ants and they

01:04:43.521 --> 01:04:45.450
just carry them to
this new nest site.

01:04:45.451 --> 01:04:48.465
And this is how they
move. And so this is like

01:04:48.466 --> 01:04:50.665
&gt;&gt; They're auditioning.

01:04:50.666 --> 01:04:51.870
&gt;&gt; Yeah, exactly.

01:04:51.871 --> 01:04:54.975
Or they're trying to
analyze the algorithm.

01:04:54.976 --> 01:04:58.590
It's actually a super
well understood algorithm.

01:04:58.591 --> 01:05:00.640
Biologists know
exactly what they're doing.

01:05:00.641 --> 01:05:02.745
They can test things
like for example,

01:05:02.746 --> 01:05:04.830
they can test if they can

01:05:04.831 --> 01:05:06.560
prevent the ants from
reaching quorum threshold.

01:05:06.561 --> 01:05:09.940
They literally do things like
put skirts around the ants,

01:05:09.941 --> 01:05:11.100
little wire skirts so they

01:05:11.101 --> 01:05:12.615
can't interact with other ants.

01:05:12.616 --> 01:05:13.870
And then they notice
that for example,

01:05:13.871 --> 01:05:15.000
they'll never reach
quorum threshold

01:05:15.001 --> 01:05:15.630
because they can never

01:05:15.631 --> 01:05:16.820
detect when they're bumping

01:05:16.821 --> 01:05:17.890
into other ants, and
things like that.

01:05:17.891 --> 01:05:19.220
So, they can really
test what they're doing.

01:05:19.221 --> 01:05:21.660
The question becomes
analyze what they're doing,

01:05:21.661 --> 01:05:23.950
why is it working with
a good probability for them,

01:05:23.951 --> 01:05:26.390
and also like why
are they doing it?

01:05:26.391 --> 01:05:28.170
How is the quorum
threshold decided?

01:05:28.171 --> 01:05:30.110
So, should the quorum
threshold increases

01:05:30.111 --> 01:05:31.190
colony size increases,

01:05:31.191 --> 01:05:33.170
or should it be independent
of the size of the colony?

01:05:33.171 --> 01:05:34.770
Should it have
something to do with

01:05:34.771 --> 01:05:37.190
the number of potential
nest sites in the area?

01:05:37.191 --> 01:05:38.945
Do they adapt it,
or is it just fixed?

01:05:38.946 --> 01:05:40.400
Answering all these
questions is really

01:05:40.401 --> 01:05:41.780
interesting questions
for biologists,

01:05:41.781 --> 01:05:44.435
and there are questions that
we have the tools to solve.

01:05:44.436 --> 01:05:46.460
&gt;&gt; So, how do

01:05:46.461 --> 01:05:49.630
you gently evaluate whether
they found a good nest?

01:05:49.631 --> 01:05:52.670
&gt;&gt; So, usually it's
in a lab setting,

01:05:52.671 --> 01:05:54.610
and there are some parameters

01:05:54.611 --> 01:05:56.440
you have to decide
what makes a good nest.

01:05:56.441 --> 01:05:58.005
But, usually you set up

01:05:58.006 --> 01:06:00.100
a nest in a lab setting
that, for example,

01:06:00.101 --> 01:06:02.205
some nest will have a closed top

01:06:02.206 --> 01:06:03.900
which is good for them
because it's more protected,

01:06:03.901 --> 01:06:05.440
some will have no top at all.

01:06:05.441 --> 01:06:07.580
Some will have a small entrance
which is easy to defend,

01:06:07.581 --> 01:06:08.740
some will have a large entrance.

01:06:08.741 --> 01:06:10.430
So you can sort of, some vague

01:06:10.431 --> 01:06:12.535
understanding of
what good means.

01:06:12.536 --> 01:06:14.775
&gt;&gt; And what happens if

01:06:14.776 --> 01:06:16.820
two quorums forms
about the same time?

01:06:16.821 --> 01:06:18.910
&gt;&gt; Yes. This is sort
of the fail case.

01:06:18.911 --> 01:06:21.145
I mean, one fail case
is getting a bad nest.

01:06:21.146 --> 01:06:23.120
The fail case that happens more

01:06:23.121 --> 01:06:24.550
often at least in
lab situations,

01:06:24.551 --> 01:06:25.620
but it doesn't
seem to happen for

01:06:25.621 --> 01:06:27.755
whatever reason in
nature is, they split.

01:06:27.756 --> 01:06:30.820
The queen can only go to
one of those colonies,

01:06:30.821 --> 01:06:33.570
so the other colony will
die out unless they move.

01:06:33.571 --> 01:06:35.405
Usually, what happens
is they do move.

01:06:35.406 --> 01:06:36.520
So, usually what happens

01:06:36.521 --> 01:06:37.930
is if the nests
are close enough,

01:06:37.931 --> 01:06:39.500
they also have a process

01:06:39.501 --> 01:06:40.870
where they notice that
there's two nests,

01:06:40.871 --> 01:06:42.450
and then they
eventually recombine.

01:06:42.451 --> 01:06:43.550
But it often takes a long time.

01:06:43.551 --> 01:06:45.815
There's not a group who are
trying to take the queen ant?

01:06:45.816 --> 01:06:47.630
&gt;&gt; Yes. You don't have
like a fight over that,

01:06:47.631 --> 01:06:48.200
because I don't think they're

01:06:48.201 --> 01:06:49.270
coordinated enough to do that.

01:06:49.271 --> 01:06:50.525
But in general,

01:06:50.526 --> 01:06:52.080
if you end up in a nest
without the queen,

01:06:52.081 --> 01:06:53.970
then that subset of
the population will just,

01:06:53.971 --> 01:06:55.665
basically, they'll die off.

01:06:55.666 --> 01:06:57.575
I mean not the
individual ants but.

01:06:57.576 --> 01:07:00.085
&gt;&gt; So, you mentioned that
they do random exploration.

01:07:00.086 --> 01:07:01.965
Is there any good
understanding call,

01:07:01.966 --> 01:07:03.670
like what is it
their random number?

01:07:03.671 --> 01:07:06.370
Generators? Or they
can use them too?

01:07:06.371 --> 01:07:08.590
&gt;&gt; I don't, so no. I mean.

01:07:08.591 --> 01:07:09.780
&gt;&gt; Right.

01:07:09.781 --> 01:07:11.970
Did you use random in the
precise? So, it's random.

01:07:11.971 --> 01:07:13.320
&gt;&gt; I use them in precise.

01:07:13.321 --> 01:07:15.835
This is the only way that
I've seen them studied.

01:07:15.836 --> 01:07:18.145
Well, as I'm getting
on the next slide,

01:07:18.146 --> 01:07:20.530
let me answer that
in one second.

01:07:20.531 --> 01:07:23.350
So, we have some work
trying to understand

01:07:23.351 --> 01:07:24.900
this quorum-virtual
strategies and when

01:07:24.901 --> 01:07:27.600
it's effective, and
why it's useful.

01:07:27.601 --> 01:07:29.670
It has close connections,

01:07:29.671 --> 01:07:32.395
not surprisingly, with
distributed consensus protocols.

01:07:32.396 --> 01:07:34.400
And then we also have
some work on zooming

01:07:34.401 --> 01:07:36.230
in on one primitive
that these ants use,

01:07:36.231 --> 01:07:38.050
which is estimating
population density.

01:07:38.051 --> 01:07:39.970
This is what they use to
test their quorum threshold.

01:07:39.971 --> 01:07:41.645
So, if there's
a high density in the nest,

01:07:41.646 --> 01:07:43.650
this means that
probably more scouts

01:07:43.651 --> 01:07:44.830
are trying to recruit there,

01:07:44.831 --> 01:07:46.530
and then they're more
likely to be in quorum,

01:07:46.531 --> 01:07:49.035
and it's a good time
to move to that nest.

01:07:49.036 --> 01:07:51.660
And so, how do they
estimate population density?

01:07:51.661 --> 01:07:53.320
Well, they walk around randomly,

01:07:53.321 --> 01:07:54.580
and they count
the number of times that

01:07:54.581 --> 01:07:55.930
they bump into another ant.

01:07:55.931 --> 01:07:57.400
The more times they
bump into another

01:07:57.401 --> 01:07:58.650
ant the higher the density,

01:07:58.651 --> 01:08:00.865
the less times
the lower the density.

01:08:00.866 --> 01:08:03.560
And what we do is, we
basically analyze this process,

01:08:03.561 --> 01:08:04.960
and give some sort
of analysis about

01:08:04.961 --> 01:08:06.645
why they're able
to analyze density,

01:08:06.646 --> 01:08:07.700
and why this suffices for

01:08:07.701 --> 01:08:09.055
something like quorum threshold.

01:08:09.056 --> 01:08:10.980
Now, our analysis assumes

01:08:10.981 --> 01:08:12.660
that there are literally
random walking agents,

01:08:12.661 --> 01:08:13.915
which is certainly not true.

01:08:13.916 --> 01:08:15.485
What you can do
to sort of try to

01:08:15.486 --> 01:08:17.440
at least test
whether the analysis.

01:08:17.441 --> 01:08:19.310
So, what some people have
done, at least to test to

01:08:19.311 --> 01:08:20.660
some degree whether the analysis

01:08:20.661 --> 01:08:22.400
holds although you would
need to do more work,

01:08:22.401 --> 01:08:25.340
is for example, take
videos of these ants,

01:08:25.341 --> 01:08:27.785
label them individually, and

01:08:27.786 --> 01:08:29.140
count the number of collisions

01:08:29.141 --> 01:08:30.625
you have between different ants,

01:08:30.626 --> 01:08:32.030
and then look at
the distributions,

01:08:32.031 --> 01:08:33.040
the movements of
these distributions,

01:08:33.041 --> 01:08:34.675
how many times you
collide with other ants.

01:08:34.676 --> 01:08:36.710
And then you can say,
is this about what you'd

01:08:36.711 --> 01:08:39.045
expect if they were walking
around in uniform, or random?

01:08:39.046 --> 01:08:40.540
So, some people
have actually done

01:08:40.541 --> 01:08:42.180
work on this, following
up on our work,

01:08:42.181 --> 01:08:43.780
and they do show that it

01:08:43.781 --> 01:08:44.830
is close to what you'd

01:08:44.831 --> 01:08:46.160
expect from a
uniform distribution.

01:08:46.161 --> 01:08:48.685
&gt;&gt; So, zero approximation
of this random assumption is

01:08:48.686 --> 01:08:50.110
&gt;&gt; Yes, exactly.

01:08:50.111 --> 01:08:51.200
So, at least to some degree

01:08:51.201 --> 01:08:52.590
this random
assumption is decent,

01:08:52.591 --> 01:08:54.400
and you could potentially
do other things.

01:08:54.401 --> 01:08:56.060
So, you can try
to test it like to

01:08:56.061 --> 01:08:59.210
some degree. I think
that's it actually.

01:08:59.211 --> 01:09:01.550
There's close connections
with social links.

01:09:01.551 --> 01:09:03.750
So, estimating
population density

01:09:03.751 --> 01:09:05.830
is not so different
from estimating area.

01:09:05.831 --> 01:09:07.420
The area that you're
spread out over,

01:09:07.421 --> 01:09:09.160
and there's close connections
estimating the size of

01:09:09.161 --> 01:09:10.910
social networks via sampling.

01:09:10.911 --> 01:09:13.270
So, we show some
connections there.

01:09:13.271 --> 01:09:15.660
So, that I'll wrap up and then
take questions in the end.

01:09:15.661 --> 01:09:17.670
So, ask one in the general area.

01:09:17.671 --> 01:09:20.090
&gt;&gt; Any kind of intervention
during the process?

01:09:20.091 --> 01:09:22.920
Like you deliberately
change the potential nest?

01:09:22.921 --> 01:09:25.230
Or you deliberately
skirt the ants?

01:09:25.231 --> 01:09:27.865
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, this is

01:09:27.866 --> 01:09:31.240
the one example I was saying
was with these metal skirts.

01:09:31.241 --> 01:09:32.570
What they'll do is
they'll let the ants

01:09:32.571 --> 01:09:34.940
run the process mostly,

01:09:34.941 --> 01:09:36.800
and then they'll somehow
restrict their movement,

01:09:36.801 --> 01:09:37.980
or restrict so that you can

01:09:37.981 --> 01:09:39.430
see whether or not
the ant will make it.

01:09:39.431 --> 01:09:40.670
They would have made
a decision, and you

01:09:40.671 --> 01:09:41.875
stopped them from
making that decision.

01:09:41.876 --> 01:09:43.580
&gt;&gt; So you stop them up when
they've already started

01:09:43.581 --> 01:09:45.590
investigating the nest,
or at the very beginning?

01:09:45.591 --> 01:09:46.880
&gt;&gt; You stop them,

01:09:46.881 --> 01:09:48.630
in this experiment
that I'm talking about,

01:09:48.631 --> 01:09:49.790
you actually stop them after

01:09:49.791 --> 01:09:51.390
they've already investigated and

01:09:51.391 --> 01:09:55.420
also recruited some other ants
to that nest and stuff.

01:09:55.421 --> 01:09:57.635
I mean, there's
a lot of really cool,

01:09:57.636 --> 01:10:00.290
I don't actually design
these experiments.

01:10:00.291 --> 01:10:01.600
This is like, we're working

01:10:01.601 --> 01:10:03.175
with people that design
these experiments.

01:10:03.176 --> 01:10:05.060
But they do a lot
of physical things.

01:10:05.061 --> 01:10:06.440
For example, there's
a lot of studying on

01:10:06.441 --> 01:10:08.310
how they can cooperatively

01:10:08.311 --> 01:10:10.770
transport large pieces of

01:10:10.771 --> 01:10:12.135
food which is something
they do a lot.

01:10:12.136 --> 01:10:14.980
In there, you can put
four sensors on this fake food,

01:10:14.981 --> 01:10:16.050
you can put four sensors,

01:10:16.051 --> 01:10:19.045
and you can try to figure out
exactly what they're doing.

01:10:19.046 --> 01:10:23.065
&gt;&gt; So, I have a question
on the previous point.

01:10:23.066 --> 01:10:24.910
So, you said many of
your algorithms for

01:10:24.911 --> 01:10:27.405
sampling, you could
distribute them.

01:10:27.406 --> 01:10:29.290
They can also have
the distributed model.

01:10:29.291 --> 01:10:32.245
So, what exactly is
the distributed model?

01:10:32.246 --> 01:10:34.930
Because, I think that

01:10:34.931 --> 01:10:37.750
given that specification
and things,

01:10:37.751 --> 01:10:38.850
I don't know whether they can be

01:10:38.851 --> 01:10:43.350
solely in distributed settings.

01:10:43.351 --> 01:10:44.970
&gt;&gt; I'm just vaguely saying that,

01:10:44.971 --> 01:10:46.930
I'm just talking
about a model where I

01:10:46.931 --> 01:10:48.740
have a number of servers.

01:10:48.741 --> 01:10:50.590
Say, they're well
connected to each other,

01:10:50.591 --> 01:10:52.900
and each of them holds
some subset of my data.

01:10:52.901 --> 01:10:54.430
It's really natural
to distribute

01:10:54.431 --> 01:10:56.325
our algorithm to
these sorts of models,

01:10:56.326 --> 01:10:57.810
because you can
compute leverage square

01:10:57.811 --> 01:10:59.465
separately on the data sets.

01:10:59.466 --> 01:11:00.820
And by this nice monitor nests,

01:11:00.821 --> 01:11:02.165
see if properly you
can really easily

01:11:02.166 --> 01:11:04.930
merge your samples together.

01:11:04.931 --> 01:11:06.290
So, I compute leverage square

01:11:06.291 --> 01:11:08.405
separately on the different
subsets of my data.

01:11:08.406 --> 01:11:10.935
I sample it down to get
some sort of small subset,

01:11:10.936 --> 01:11:12.340
and then I can recombine.

01:11:12.341 --> 01:11:13.680
When I recombine
in my laboratories,

01:11:13.681 --> 01:11:14.700
I'll only decrease
and then I can

01:11:14.701 --> 01:11:16.260
sample them out basically.

01:11:16.261 --> 01:11:18.350
So, I'm not talking about
a model for example,

01:11:18.351 --> 01:11:19.980
where every single data point

01:11:19.981 --> 01:11:21.800
is its own server, or
something like this.

01:11:21.801 --> 01:11:23.515
&gt;&gt; Right. I mean,

01:11:23.516 --> 01:11:26.290
you worked with Defiance and
they have really specific.

01:11:26.291 --> 01:11:27.350
&gt;&gt; Yes, I'm not talking about

01:11:27.351 --> 01:11:30.740
those recent models you have.

01:11:30.741 --> 01:11:32.150
So, people that
I work with yeah.

01:11:32.151 --> 01:11:33.925
&gt;&gt; But, I think that

01:11:33.926 --> 01:11:36.810
specification in the
distributed model is quite an.

01:11:36.811 --> 01:11:39.260
&gt;&gt; I think this is now known.

01:11:39.261 --> 01:11:40.910
There's some recent
progress on this

01:11:40.911 --> 01:11:45.430
using some random
spanner type methods.

01:11:45.431 --> 01:11:47.590
Either way, it is
important questions.

01:11:47.591 --> 01:11:48.325
But I'm not thinking about

01:11:48.326 --> 01:11:49.480
those sorts of models currently.

01:11:49.481 --> 01:11:54.080
&gt;&gt; I guess, let's take the rest
of the questions offline.

01:11:54.081 --> 01:11:56.320
And thank the speaker.

